<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Talib ibn Abd al-Muttalib</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 535–619</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Abu Talib</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Talib ibn Abd al-Muttalib was an uncle of the Islamic prophet Muhammad, the father of Ali, and the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hashim">Banu Hashim</a> clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. He took in Muhammad after the death of his grandfather Abd al-Muttalib and, as clan chief, protected him when other Quraysh leaders pressed him to silence his nephew, including during a three-year boycott of the Banu Hashim. He died around 619, within a month of Khadija. His religious status at death is disputed: most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> scholars hold that he did not accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, while Shia scholars hold that he was a believer who concealed his faith to protect Muhammad.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu Talib was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in about 535 CE, a son of the Hashimite chief Abd al-Muttalib and a brother of Muhammad's father Abdullah, who had died before Muhammad's birth. After the death of Muhammad's mother Aminah bint Wahab, the child was cared for by Abd al-Muttalib, who died when Muhammad was eight. The oldest uncle, Al-Harith, was not wealthy enough to take him in, and Abu Talib did so despite his own poverty. He inherited the position of tribal chieftain and the offices of Siqaya and Rifada, supplying food and drink to pilgrims, though he had to let his brother Abbas take over the duty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Abu Talib is remembered as a gifted poet, and many verses supporting Muhammad are attributed to him. After a severe drought Muhammad took charge of one of Abu Talib's children and persuaded Abbas to do the same: Abbas chose Ja'far and Muhammad chose Ali.</p><p>As leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hashim">Banu Hashim</a>, Abu Talib protected Muhammad, in a society where a man without tribal protection could be killed with impunity. When Muhammad began preaching, other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> clans pressed Abu Talib to silence him. He refused, even at the cost of a rift with the Quraysh, who offered to give him the youth 'Umarah ibn al-Walid in exchange. They then won the support of other tribes for a boycott of trade and marriage with the Banu Hashim, which began seven years after Muhammad's first revelation and lasted three years. His brother Abu Lahab sided with the Quraysh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Abu Talib died around 619, at more than 80 years of age. Khadija died within a month of him, and the year is known as the Year of Sorrow for Muhammad. Abu Lahab succeeded as chief of the family but did not protect Muhammad, who is quoted as saying that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> never harmed him so much as after Abu Talib's death. The early Muslims then moved to Abyssinia and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. His memory is influenced by the political aims of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Shia Muslims, and it is reported in Sunni Islam that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> 28:56 was revealed concerning him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Talib ibn Abd al-Muttalib” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
